---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ScreenCalculator from '../../components/ScreenCalculator.tsx';

const title = 'Screen & projection';
const description =
  'Throw distance, on-screen brightness in nits and foot-lamberts, AT fabric loss, scope via zoom or anamorphic lens, lens shift and seating distance – calculated to scale for your room.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How do I convert lumens to nits or foot-lamberts?',
    a: 'Luminance (nits, cd/m²) = lumens × gain × (1 − fabric loss) × (1 − lens loss) ÷ (π × lit area in m²). One foot-lambert is 3.426 nits. Use a calibrated, measured lumen figure rather than the spec sheet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How bright should a home cinema image be?',
    a: 'Cinemas use 14 foot-lamberts, about 48 nits (set out in the SMPTE ST 431-1 standard). Many home cinemas aim for that in a dark room. For HDR films it can be brighter. Below 12 foot-lamberts the picture looks dull.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Zoom or anamorphic lens for a scope screen?',
    a: 'With zoom the picture is simply made bigger. The black bars at the top and bottom land off the screen, losing about a quarter of the light. An add-on lens stretches the picture sideways and uses all the light, but costs some light and money itself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the right seating distance?',
    a: 'A common tip is that the picture should fill 30 to 40 degrees of your view (30° from an older SMPTE guideline, 36 to 40° from THX). Many people deliberately sit closer. The calculator shows you the matching distances for your picture width.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Plan My Cinema – Screen & projection',
    url: 'https://plan-my-cinema.com/en/screen-projection/',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' },
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base lang="en" page="screen" title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Screen <span>&amp;</span> projection</h1>
    <p>How far away the projector has to be, how bright the picture gets and where you should sit – matched to your room.</p>
  </div>

  <ScreenCalculator client:load lang="en" />

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What is this about?</h2>
    <p>
      A projector throws a picture onto the screen. This calculator tells you how far away the projector has to be, how bright the picture will be
      and how far from the screen you should sit. You enter your room, your screen and your projector – the page works out the rest.
    </p>

    <h3>How bright will the picture be?</h3>
    <p>
      A projector makes a certain amount of light, measured in <strong>lumens</strong>. That light is spread over the whole screen. The bigger the
      screen, the thinner it gets spread – and the darker the picture. How bright the picture really is, is given in <strong>nits</strong> or
      <strong>foot-lamberts (fL)</strong>. Cinemas use 14 fL (about 48 nits). Below that the picture soon looks dull; for HDR films it can be brighter.
    </p>
    <p>
      Careful: manufacturers usually measure the lumens on the data sheet in the brightest picture mode. With natural colours there is often a lot
      less. If you pick a projector from our list, we therefore only use 70 % of the manufacturer’s figure. If you have measured your own, enter it.
    </p>

    <h3>A screen with holes (AT fabric)</h3>
    <p>
      Some screens have tiny holes or are woven. That lets the speakers stand behind the screen, so the sound comes right out of the picture. In
      return, some light goes through the holes and is lost. How much is in your fabric’s data sheet.
    </p>

    <h3>Widescreen: zoom or add-on lens</h3>
    <p>
      Many films are wider than a TV. For an extra-wide screen (called “scope”) there are two ways. With <strong>zoom</strong> the projector simply
      makes the picture bigger until the film fills the whole width. The black bars at the top and bottom then land off the screen – about a quarter
      of the light is lost. An <strong>add-on lens</strong> (anamorphic lens) stretches the picture sideways instead. That uses all the light, but the
      lens itself costs some light and money.
    </p>

    <h3>How far away does the projector have to be?</h3>
    <p>
      That depends on your projector’s <strong>throw ratio</strong>: distance divided by picture width. With a throw ratio of 1.5, the projector sits
      4.5 metres away for a 3-metre-wide picture. If it has a zoom, there is a range from – to.
    </p>

    <h3>Lens shift: moving the picture without bending it</h3>
    <p>
      If the projector isn’t exactly centred in front of the screen – say it hangs from the ceiling – <strong>lens shift</strong> can move the
      picture up, down or sideways without making it crooked. The calculator checks whether your projector has enough room to do that.
    </p>

    <h3>How far away should I sit?</h3>
    <p>
      Sit close and the picture fills more of what you see – like in a cinema. Sit far away and it looks smaller. A common tip is that the picture
      should fill about 30 to 40 degrees of your view. There is no right or wrong, though: many home cinema fans deliberately sit closer. The
      calculator shows you the angle at your seat.
    </p>

    <h3>How exact is this?</h3>
    <p>
      The maths is right for an ordinary, evenly bright screen and for the figures on the data sheet. Every projector and every screen is a little
      different – in the end what counts is how the picture looks in your room.
    </p>

    <details class="tech">
      <summary>For the technically minded: how we calculate</summary>


    <h3>Lumens are not brightness</h3>
    <p>What you see is luminance on the screen. It depends on the projector’s light output, the lit area and the screen gain:</p>
    <p><code>nits = lumens × gain × (1 − fabric loss) × (1 − lens loss) ÷ (π × area in m²)</code></p>
    <p>
      One foot-lambert (fL) equals 3.426 nits. The formula assumes a perfectly diffusing screen; gain describes how far the real screen departs from
      that on axis. High-gain screens get dimmer off-centre, which this calculator does not model.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use a calibrated figure. Spec-sheet lumens are usually measured in the brightest picture mode and tend to sit well above what is left after
      calibrating to D65. When you pick a projector from the list we prefill 70 % of the rated output – a rough assumption. If you have a
      measurement, enter it.
    </p>

    <h3>Brightness rating</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>fL</th><th>nits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>below 12</td><td>below 41</td><td>too dim, flat picture</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12–16</td><td>41–55</td><td>SDR cinema reference (dark room)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16–24</td><td>55–82</td><td>bright SDR with some headroom</td></tr>
        <tr><td>24 and up</td><td>82 and up</td><td>headroom for HDR tone mapping</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The bands above 16 fL are our own classification, not a standard. The 14 fL cinema reference (48 cd/m², tolerance ±3.5 cd/m²) is set by SMPTE ST 431-1 for digital
      cinema projection. There is no fixed target for projected HDR – more light simply gives tone mapping more room.
    </p>

    <h3>AT fabric</h3>
    <p>
      Acoustically transparent screens let sound through perforations or a weave, and lose light in the process. How much depends heavily on the
      fabric – take the figure from your fabric’s spec sheet.
    </p>

    <h3>Scope: zoom or anamorphic lens</h3>
    <p>
      Almost every projector has a 16:9 panel. For a scope screen (2.35:1 to 2.40:1) you have two options. <strong>Zoom:</strong> enlarge the
      16:9 image until the film fills the screen width; the black bars land above and below the screen, taking about 24 % of the light at 2.35:1
      with them. <strong>Anamorphic lens:</strong> the projector stretches the image vertically across the whole panel and the lens widens it
      again. All panel pixels and all the light end up on screen, minus the lens’s own loss, which you can enter. Note that throw ratio then
      refers to the <em>unstretched</em> image width (image height × 16/9), so the projector can sit closer.
    </p>

    <h3>Throw distance and lens shift</h3>
    <p>
      Distance = throw ratio × image width. Manufacturers measure from the lens front or from the chassis, so leave some margin.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lens shift moves the image without keystone distortion. The calculator works out how far the image centre sits from the lens axis, as a
      percentage of projected image height or width – the way most spec sheets state it. With scope via zoom, the full 16:9 image height counts.
      Many projectors cannot use full vertical and horizontal shift at the same time; the calculator applies a simple diamond-shaped check. Your
      spec sheet has the real limits.
    </p>

    <h3>Seating distance</h3>
    <p>
      Horizontal viewing angle = 2 × arctan(image width ÷ (2 × distance)). Two figures come up again and again: the 30° minimum goes back to
      SMPTE EG 18-1994, which SMPTE withdrew in 2003. THX advises cinema designers to plan for 36° from the farthest seat, and THX’s rule of
      thumb for TVs (diagonal = 0.835 × seating distance) works out at about 40° for 16:9. Many enthusiasts sit closer for scope. There is no
      single right answer – resolution, seating rows and taste all play a part.
    </p>
    </details>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
